        <h3>Red Flags That Signal a Risky Supplier</h3>
        <p>Some warning signs justify walking away, others justify digging much harder. No legitimate factory has a reason to refuse a live video walkthrough of the floor, so refusal belongs in the first group, as does pressure to wire a large bulk deposit before any sample exists — a sample fee is standard practice, a 50% prepayment on an unseen product is not. Pricing that sits far outside the band everyone else quotes deserves the same suspicion: if three quotes cluster in one band and a fourth lands at roughly half that, something is being cut, whether fabric weight, labor, or the factory itself</p>
        <p>The softer signals accumulate. Shifting answers on MOQ and lead time are tolerable once; repeated evasion is a pattern. Catalog images with no production photos prove nothing, since anyone can download product shots. Check the registered business address, and treat a residential or missing address as a reason to verify before proceeding. Finally, a proforma invoice with no written terms leaves both sides exposed, which is why a real contract matters more on a first order than on a fifth</p>
        <p>Want a second opinion on a supplier's answers? <a href="/contact-us/">Talk to our team</a> and we will walk through the checklist with you</p>
        <h2 id="step-4">Step 4 — Sampling &amp; Communication: Testing Before You Commit</h2>
        <p>Sampling is the only reliable test of real capability. A good sampling round proves four things at once: tech-pack compliance, actual fabric weight, construction cleanliness, and color accuracy</p>
        <h3>How to Evaluate a Sample (Checklist)</h3>
        <p>Judge the sample against your tech pack, not against your memory of what you asked for</p>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Measurements</strong> Measure every point on the POM sheet and record deviations. A 1 cm variance on chest width is usually workable; 3 cm means the pattern is wrong</li><li><strong>Fabric and color</strong> Weigh the garment against the specified GSM and check consistency between body and sleeve panels. Compare color against the approved lab dip under daylight, not office fluorescents</li><li><strong>Stitching quality</strong> Inspect the hood seam, armhole, cuff and hem ribbing attachment, and any topstitching. Look for skipped stitches, puckering, loose thread ends</li><li><strong>Decoration, hardware and trims</strong> Confirm print placement, size, Pantone match, and adhesion; for embroidery, check stitch density and backing. Then verify drawcord type and length, eyelet finish, zipper travel, and label placement</li><li><strong>Fit on body</strong> Put it on a fit model or yourself. Pattern problems show up on a body, never on a hanger</li></ul>
        <p>Photograph every issue and write it up. Send one consolidated feedback document instead of a stream of separate messages, because factories move faster against a single revision list. Our <a href="/our-services/">sampling process</a> is built on exactly that: one round of consolidated feedback, one revision, then approval</p>
        <p>In YAUUM programs we work to a defined cadence: one proto sample, one correction round, and a pre-production sample signed off before fabric is cut. During bulk, inspection points sit at in-line cutting, mid-production sewing, and final pack-out, with ±1 cm held on principal girth measurements and ±0.5 cm on ribbing and sleeve length</p>
