        <h3>A Simple Inquiry Email Template</h3>
        <p>Fill the brackets and send. The supplier gets everything needed to quote accurately on the first reply</p>
        <pre class="ar-pre"><code>Subject: Hoodie production inquiry - [Your Brand] - [Quantity] pcs
        Hi [Name],
We are [Brand], a [streetwear / casual / sportswear] brand selling in [market],
looking for a manufacturing partner for a hoodie program.
        - Style: [pullover / half-zip / full-zip]
- Fabric: [GSM], [composition]
- Fit: [regular / relaxed / oversized / drop shoulder]
- Decoration: [screen print / embroidery / DTG / other]
- Sizes: [range], size curve attached
- Quantity: [pcs per color], [number of colors]
- Target delivery: [month/year]
        Tech pack and reference sample available on request. Please confirm MOQ per color,
sample lead time and fee, bulk lead time, FOB price range, payment terms, and
whether you can share a short factory video.
        [Name], [Brand]</code></pre>
        <h2 id="step-5">Step 5 — Pricing, MOQ &amp; Lead Time: What to Expect</h2>
        <p>Hoodie pricing turns on five variables. Any supplier who quotes a flat number without asking about them is guessing</p>
        <p>Fabric is the largest single cost line, driven by GSM and fiber composition: moving from 280 GSM to 400 GSM raises unit cost materially, while swapping 100% cotton for a 65/35 cotton/poly blend pushes it back down. Decoration is priced by method — embroidery typically per 1,000 stitches, screen printing per color per location, DTG with economics that change sharply by volume. Order quantity spreads setup cost across more units, and the curve is steepest between 100 and 500 pieces before it flattens. Construction complexity adds on top, since full-zip hoodies carry a zipper, extra paneling, and extra stitching operations, and drop-shoulder or oversized blocks consume more fabric. Trims and packaging close the list: custom woven labels, hangtags, poly bags, and retail-ready presentation all add per-unit cost that most retail buyers will not let you skip</p>
        <p>For planning purposes, hoodie MOQs commonly sit between 100 and 500 pieces per color across many factories. Per-unit FOB pricing tracks your fabric, decoration, and volume combination rather than any single published rate, and it swings widely by factory and region, so treat any range you see as a planning input and not a quote. The detail sits on our page covering <a href="/hoodie-manufacturer/#commercial">hoodie manufacturing MOQ and lead times</a></p>
        <p><strong>Lead time and payment planning:</strong></p>
        <p>Sampling and bulk production run on separate clocks: sample development, revision where needed, bulk production, QC, then shipping, with transit depending on destination and mode (sea freight slower and cheaper than air). On the payment side, typical structures pair a deposit at order confirmation with a balance before or after shipment, sometimes with a third-party inspection milestone between the two. Whatever you agree, put it in writing, and on larger orders hold the balance payment until a third-party inspection report clears</p>
        <h2 id="mistakes">Common Mistakes Buyers Make When Sourcing Hoodies</h2>
        <ul class="ar-list"><li><strong>Comparing quotes that aren't comparable</strong> Two prices only line up when fabric, construction, and decoration match. Normalize every quote against the same tech pack</li><li><strong>Skipping sampling to save time</strong> Those two weeks saved routinely become two months of rework. Never release bulk production without a signed-off sample in hand</li><li><strong>Ignoring compliance requirements</strong> Textile labeling, fiber content disclosure, and chemical restriction rules vary by market. State your target market and compliance needs in the first inquiry, not after cutting</li><li><strong>Vague communication</strong> "Make it like the photo" is not a specification. Work from a tech pack plus a reference sample, and define acceptable quality limits, measurement tolerances, and defect thresholds in the PO</li></ul>
